# .gitignore — PUBLIC governance repository
#
# Two jobs. First, keep build and cache artifacts out of a repo that is meant to be
# readable source. Second, and more important: make it IMPOSSIBLE to commit a
# machine-local governance config by accident.
#
# The invariant these rules protect (see README.md "Placeholder convention"):
#   a TRACKED file carries a placeholder or an env lookup;
#   the REAL value lives in a machine-local, gitignored config and is never synced.
#
# check-no-pii.sh is the gate that catches a real value that slips into a tracked file.
# The rules below are the belt to that scanner's braces: they stop the whole config file
# from being staged in the first place.

# --- Machine-local governance config — NEVER commit these -------------------
# Each one is the designated home for a real value that a tracked file only
# references by placeholder or env lookup.
.governance-local.env
.governance-mirrors
.wa-bridge.json
.pii-names

# Same files wherever they land in the tree, not just at the root.
**/.governance-local.env
**/.governance-mirrors
**/.wa-bridge.json
**/.pii-names

# --- Secrets and environment ------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
credentials.json
creds.json

# --- Language and build caches ----------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.pytest_cache/
node_modules/
.npm/

# --- OS cruft ---------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
._*
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Editor / IDE -----------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# --- Logs, backups and scratch ----------------------------------------------
*.log
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.orig
*.rej
*.tmp
tmp/

# --- Escape hatch -----------------------------------------------------------
# Templates and examples are the documented, placeholder-only counterparts of the
# machine-local files above. They are exactly what a new contributor needs, so they
# must stay committable even when a rule above would have caught the name.
!*.example
!*.template
!*.sample

# --- Appended 2026-09-01 (arming pass) --------------------------------------
# check-no-pii.sh names FIVE machine-local files in its header; four were already
# covered above, this one was not. It holds real proper nouns (people, projects,
# clients) used as a denylist — precisely the content this repo must never carry.
# Verified with: git check-ignore -v .governance-pii-denylist
.governance-pii-denylist
**/.governance-pii-denylist

# --- Appended 2026-09-09: this repo's OWN governance journal is LOCAL ---------
# The framework is now governed by itself (/init-governance, 2026-09-09), which raises a
# question the other governed projects never face: this repo is PUBLIC and DISTRIBUTED.
#
# Two reasons its journal stays out of git, not one:
#   1. Content. PLAN/MEMORY/HANDOFF record the OWNER's working state and session history.
#      That is data about the operator, not part of the product, and the product is exactly
#      install.sh + bundle/ + README.md + verify.sh.
#   2. The canonical-path guard. `canonical_working_copy` must be a LITERAL path for
#      canonical-cwd-check.sh to compare against (it has no env-lookup form, measured
#      2026-09-09). Committing a literal path would put a machine-local path in a public
#      file AND fire a false "WRONG WORKING COPY" alarm at every person who clones this repo.
#      Keeping the file untracked removes both problems at the source rather than papering
#      over either.
#
# Consequence, stated so nobody rediscovers it as a bug: a fresh clone of this repo is
# UNGOVERNED by design. Whoever wants a journal here runs /init-governance themselves and
# gets their own local one, with their own path.
CLAUDE.md
docs/context/
Plans/

# Google Drive sync scratch dir. Appears at the root of any repo Drive is syncing.
.tmp.driveupload/

# --- Appended 2026-09-18: ~/.claude-shaped secrets, defensively ----------------
# WHY A SECOND, OVERLAPPING LAYER. The sync hook already refuses to copy a contaminated file
# into bundle/, and .githooks/ carries a pre-commit/pre-push PII gate. Both are real, and
# neither is a reason to skip this: those two protect the SYNC path, while a human working in
# this clone can `git add` anything at all. These entries are shaped after the files that
# actually live in a ~/.claude tree, because that is what gets copied here by hand at 2am.
#
# Line by line, since a rule nobody understands gets deleted:
.credentials.json
**/.credentials.json
#   ^ the existing `credentials.json` rule (line 34) is a LITERAL and does NOT match the
#     leading-dot name (MEASURED 2026-09-18). Claude Code's OAuth tokens live in the dotted one.
*.local.md
*.local.json
*.local.sh
*.local.env
**/config.local.json
#   ^ formalises the `.local.` convention already in use for machine-local companions to
#     tracked files. MEASURED: one such file carries a full name, a GitHub login and a Slack
#     user id on its FIRST line. The same pattern is now enforced in sync-governance-copies.sh,
#     so "it is named .local" means the same thing to git and to the sync.
settings.json
settings.json.*
*.backup-*
#   ^ settings.json is NEVER a publishable artefact — the shipped template is
#     bundle/settings-hooks.json. MEASURED 2026-09-18: 16 settings.json.bak-* copies and 8 more
#     under backups/ each contained a plaintext credential on a command line. One `git add -A`
#     in the wrong directory is the whole threat model.
*.jsonl
#   ^ history.jsonl, wa-inbox.jsonl, slack-inbox.jsonl — verbatim conversation and inbox state.
sessions/
session-env/
projects/
file-history/
shell-snapshots/
uploads/
daemon/
state/
#   ^ Claude Code runtime state. projects/ alone is ~947 MB of session transcripts (MEASURED
#     2026-09-18) containing whatever was ever pasted into a session.

# --- THE ESCAPE HATCH MUST BE LAST -------------------------------------------
# gitignore resolves by LAST MATCHING PATTERN, so these negations only work below every rule
# they need to override. They were at lines 70-72 with ~35 lines of rules after them, which
# meant `settings.json.*` above would have swallowed `settings.json.example`. Anything added
# after this point must be added ABOVE it.
!*.example
!*.template
!*.sample
